{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/sh -lc \"rg -n 'segment|packed|polynom|cheby|Taylor|exp2' /tmp/research_history/episode_9_continuing_notes.md /tmp/research_history/episode_5_continuing_notes.md /tmp/research_history/episode_4_continuing_notes.md\"", "aggregated_output": "/tmp/research_history/episode_5_continuing_notes.md:46:Start from the exact retained source and profile it before changing the next bottleneck. Matrix construction, point kernel scaling/reductions and the remaining padded class operations are likely the next limits. General grouping, lower precision, class subsampling, weighted barycenters and structured polynomial coupling have been explored and did not improve retained official performance. A cheaper fused reduction implementation or geometry reuse may help, but preserve the canonical finite annealing recipe and measure the full protocol. Prior episode4 Taylor coupling degrees1-4 were too inaccurate or expensive; do not repeat without a materially different approximation.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_9_continuing_notes.md:37:- **498/499, unpadded Torch segmented/scatter class reductions:** all 30 pass with base accuracy. Means0.0608/0.0719 seconds are slower; reject for speed. Prior segmented NumPy failures were already covered in episodes4/6.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_9_continuing_notes.md:40:- **508–511/520–524, twenty unique supports and setup combinations:** packed global geometry, unique means/norms, repeated bmm geometry and in-place class assembly all pass. One class first-cumulant stage passes with worst0.007173; two first-cumulant stages525 fail11 cases (0.013614). Unique setup is not consistently faster than original means.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_9_continuing_notes.md:57:- Next research should begin from the selected exact source and profile it. Kernel dispatch, matrix layout and cost-energy factorization are productive exact mechanisms. More aggressive finite-schedule truncation, frozen class graphs, Gaussian class geometry, generic grouping and polynomial coupling already have extensive failures or slow controls in prior notes. Read those summaries before revisiting them.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_4_continuing_notes.md:27:- Direct dense exponentials use exp2(z*log2(e)). Two Torch threads, flush-denormal flag set before thread initialization.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_4_continuing_notes.md:31:-126-131: vectorized class setup, first-distance sharing, global distance sharing, packed class geometry, in-place coupling energy/mass. All 30 local-valid, worst nRMSE about .0039615. Sharing geometry is accurate; gather/copy costs offset some savings.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_4_continuing_notes.md:34:-141-146: global geometry, vectorized setup, pow4, exp2, in-place coupling balance, streamed hard reductions. All valid. Single pow4 much slower than two squares.145 became the next measured best. Streamed hard transforms had little effect because coarse point schedules rarely enter that branch.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_4_continuing_notes.md:35:-147-156: separate/both final-buffer mutation, streamed final point transform, combinations of geometry sharing, matrix scaling, exp2. All valid; local speed changes were noisy.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_4_continuing_notes.md:37:-161-165: combined vectorized setup, matrix scaling, final buffer reuse and exp2; precomputed feature norms; addmm features; baddbmm classes; both fused geometries. All local 30-valid. Fresh round4 medians:145 .07841,151 .06501,153 .07443,155 .06752,161 .06829,162 .06322,163 .06543,165 .06457.162 and165 received complete official evaluations; choose by reported raw above.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_4_continuing_notes.md:38:-166-168: structured Taylor expansion of exp(centered feature dot/beta) to degree1/2/3 with exact radial and empirical label factors. Grouped polynomial moments replace dense kernel applications and energy contraction; all supports retained. Fail30/27/19 cases, worst nRMSE .04324/.03411/.01691.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_4_continuing_notes.md:39:-169-170: class-wise matrix energy contractions accelerate degree2/3 Taylor variants but preserve the same failures.171 degree4 passes27/30, worst .0110019202, local mean .28121. Reject structured polynomial compression: insufficient accuracy at cheap degrees, slower than dense coupling at higher degrees.\n/tmp/research_history/episode_4_continuing_notes.md:45:-For a fresh episode, start from the exact measured best. Profile it before choosing the next bottleneck. Remaining point-dual kernel recurrence and class symmetric transforms likely dominate. Possible next hypothesis: group class pairs by support size while retaining Torch contiguous reductions, reducing padding and dense working sets. The full NumPy segmented solver and Taylor compression were slower; repeating them is unlikely useful. Public nRMSE margin is about .006.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
