{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_35", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/sh -lc \"cat > /tmp/research_history/episode11_build_round4.py <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,hashlib\nroot=Path('/tmp/research_history');base=(root/'651_episode11_class_persistent_fold_final_delta/method.py').read_text();entries=[]\ndef save(num,name,src,h):\n d=root/(str(num)+'_episode11_'+name);d.mkdir(exist_ok=True);(d/'method.py').write_text(src)\n entries.append({'name':d.name,'sha256':hashlib.sha256(src.encode()).hexdigest(),'hypothesis':h})\na=base.index('def _dual');b=base.index('def _label_cost');pt=base[a:b]\nold='''                if abs(q-4.)<1e-5:K.square_().square_()\n                else:K.pow_(q)\n                sums=K.sum(0)'''\nnew='''                if abs(q-4.)<1e-5:\n                    sums=torch.linalg.vector_norm(K,ord=4,dim=0).pow_(4)\n                else:\n                    K.pow_(q);sums=K.sum(0)'''\nassert old in pt\nsave(659,'point_last_fourth_norm',base[:a]+pt.replace(old,new)+base[b:],'Fuse final point fourth powers and column reduction using the native fourth norm; last kernel is unused afterward.')\nnew='''                if abs(q-4.)<1e-5:\n                    K.square_()\n                    sums=torch.linalg.vector_norm(K,ord=2,dim=0).square_()\n                else:\n                    K.pow_(q);sums=K.sum(0)'''\nsave(660,'point_last_square_second_norm',base[:a]+pt.replace(old,new)+base[b:],'One explicit square followed by a fused square-and-sum second norm replaces two squares followed by a sum.')\n# Keep the final norm in logarithms, cancelling the norm's root in the final reference update.\nold2='''                if abs(q-4.)<1e-5:K.square_().square_()\n                else:K.pow_(q)\n                sums=K.sum(0)\n                if float(sums.min())>0.:\n                    logmass=sums.log()+logfactor'''\nnew2='''                if abs(q-4.)<1e-5:\n                    norm=torch.linalg.vector_norm(K,ord=4,dim=0)\n                    sums=norm\n                    logsum=norm.log()*4.\n                else:\n                    K.pow_(q);sums=K.sum(0);logsum=sums.log()\n                if float(sums.min())>0.:\n                    logmass=logsum+logfactor'''\nassert old2 in pt\nsave(661,'point_last_fourth_norm_log',base[:a]+pt.replace(old2,new2)+base[b:],'Keep the fourth norm in log space and multiply log mass by four; avoid root reversal and improve dynamic range.')\nnew2=new2.replace('norm=torch.linalg.vector_norm(K,ord=4,dim=0)','K.square_()\\\\n                    norm=torch.linalg.vector_norm(K,ord=2,dim=0)').replace('logsum=norm.log()*4.','logsum=norm.log()*2.')\nsave(662,'point_last_second_norm_log',base[:a]+pt.replace(old2,new2)+base[b:],'Second norm after one square, with exact log cancellation of the square root.')\n# Compare norm reduction to stable base in the increment-combination implementation.\ns=(root/'656_episode11_point_recurrence_residual_increments/method.py').read_text();pa=s.index('def _dual');pb=s.index('def _label_cost')\nsave(663,'point_second_norm_log_increments',s[:pa]+s[pa:pb].replace(old2,new2)+s[pb:],'Combine recurrence dual increments and final norm reduction; both preserve finite annealing geometry.')\n# Preserve kernel as a square root, reducing marginals with norm2; provides a range/dispatch control.\ns=pt\ns=s.replace('R.mul_(-1.4426950408889634/eps).exp2_()','R.mul_(-.7213475204444817/eps).exp2_()')\ns=s.replace('rs=R.sum(1);cs=R.sum(0)','rs=torch.linalg.vector_norm(R,ord=2,dim=1).square_();cs=torch.linalg.vector_norm(R,ord=2,dim=0).square_()')\ns=s.replace('K.mul_((m/rs).sqrt()[:,None])','K.mul_((m/rs).sqrt_().sqrt_()[:,None])')\ns=s.replace('.mul_((n/cs).sqrt()[None,:])','.mul_((n/cs).sqrt_().sqrt_()[None,:])')\ns=s.replace('sums=K.sum(0)','sums=torch.linalg.vector_norm(K,ord=2,dim=0).square_()')\ns=s.replace('cs=K.sum(0)','cs=torch.linalg.vector_norm(K,ord=2,dim=0).square_()')\ns=s.replace('rs=K.sum(1);cs=torch.linalg.vector_norm(K,ord=2,dim=0).square_()','rs=torch.linalg.vector_norm(K,ord=2,dim=1).square_();cs=torch.linalg.vector_norm(K,ord=2,dim=0).square_()')\nsave(664,'point_sqrt_kernel_norm_marginals',base[:a]+s+base[b:],'Store the square root of the point kernel, compute marginals with fused norm2 squared, and adapt balancing factors to fourth roots.')\n(root/'episode11_round4_manifest.json').write_text(json.dumps(entries,indent=2));print(json.dumps(entries,indent=2))\nPY\npython /tmp/research_history/episode11_build_round4.py\ncat > /tmp/research_history/episode11_derivations.md <<'MD'\n# Episode 11 mechanisms\n\nAll mechanisms derive geometry from the supplied arrays and preserve every first-batch empirical class support and every point batch. Local quality diagnostics use own canonical public-array computations saved by earlier episodes. They never access protected targets.\n\n## Persistent hard class reduced costs\nAt the first globally hard class stage define \"'`R = C - f[:,None] - g[None,:]`. A parallel hard update has residual increments `df = rowmin(R) - eps*log(1/m)` and `dg = colmin(R) - eps*log(1/n)`. Thus `f_new=f+.5*df`, `g_new=g+.5*dg`, and `R_new=R-.5*df[:,None]-.5*dg[None,:]`. Keeping R across the contiguous hard suffix eliminates full dense residual allocations and subtraction of large absolute duals at every stage. Active/mask controls are preserved. The final reference extrapolation is `g += .25*dg`, so the last residual shift can combine `.5` and `.25` into `.75*dg`. Final objective is unchanged: mean(f+rowmin(R)-.1*lb)+mean(g+colmin(R)-.1*la). Variations include ordinary in-place updates, fused rank-two BMM, and scalar-only mean-dual tracking.\n\n## Rank-two residual construction\n`f[:,None]+g[None,:] = [f,ones]  @  [ones,g]`. Torch addmm/baddbmm applies the additive residual in one native call. Column-major class storage uses the transposed product `[g,ones]  @  [ones,f]`. This saves dense passes in principle but multiplication dispatch can cost more than broadcasting. Keep controls with separate output allocation and in-place output aliasing.\n\n## One-sided padded class geometry\nSort actual reference supports by observed labels and retain only candidate-side padding. Potentials f have `(candidate_label,candidate_point,reference_label)` shape, g have `(candidate_label,actual_reference_point)` shape. Segmented row transforms operate on contiguous reference label segments. Native column reductions operate over candidate points, eliminating one of the two segmented reductions used in episode 9. This layout removes reference padding but still needs dense expansion of reference-label-specific candidate potentials.\n\n## Direct dual increments\nWhenever transformed dual `ft=f+shift-eps*(log(rowmass)+lb)` is immediately followed by `df=ft-f`, compute df directly. For point kernels the analogous update is `f += -.5*eps*(log(rowmass)+lb)`, and similarly g. Fallbacks retain stable direct transforms. Only floating reduction/addition order changes.\n\n## Fused final point powers/reduction\nIn the reference-only final stage, K after row balancing is raised to q=4, then summed by column. The final K is never used again. `sum(K**4) = norm_4(K)**4 = norm_2(K**2)**2`. Native vector norms may fuse a power and reduction, avoiding dense write/read passes. The root can cancel directly in logs: `log(sum(K**4))=4*log(norm_4(K))=2*log(norm_2(K**2))`. Sum positivity retains the stable logsumexp fallback. A square-root kernel storage control adapts dual balancing factors to fourth roots and obtains all marginals via norm2 squared.\nMD'", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
